In base-64, if you have `A` apples, you have no apples.  
If you have `9` apples in base-64, you have decimal `61` apples.  
And if you have `10` apples in base-64, you have decimal `3444` apples.  
It seems weird because 0 through 9 have completely different meanings in base-64.  
Write out the columns.

    4096 | 64 | 1

Add the values:

    4096 | 64 | 1
       0 |  1 | 0

The trick here is to respect the order of the symbols used in base-64.  
In base-10, `0` is the first symbol and represents nothing.  
In base-64, `A` is the first symbol and represents nothing.  
In base-64, the symbol `0` is the 53rd symbol in the number system. It represents the decimal quantity `52`.  
As long as that "order" is respected, all the converting is the same.  

With `10` in base-64, we're saying the first column has 1 groups of 64 (or decimal 53 groups of 64).  
And the second column has 0 groups of of 1 (or 52 groups of 1).  

   2nd column   1st column
   (64 * 53)  + 52         = 3444

So yes, `10` in base-64 is the same as `3444` in decimal.  

