When you're working with Dlls, you have to understand what they want for each parameter.  
Each parameter is going to expect a different set of bytes to work with.  
In AHK, all variables are the same size. We don't worry about int and uint and char and all the different sized smaller bytes.  
When working with DllCalls, you DO have to worry about those.  

To recap, a bit is a single binary digit. `1` or `0`.  
A bytes is 8 bits: `00000000` to `11111111`  
That means 8 columns.  
You can make a cheat sheet or you can do it the math way and say `2 ^ 8`.  
Either way, you realize 8 bytes can represent anywhere from 0 to 255

An `int` is 4 bytes.  

There is an [data type range MSDN page](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/cpp/data-type-ranges?view=msvc-170&redirectedfrom=MSDN) that covers a lot of the common data types.  


With DllCalls, we use different sizes of memory.  
For an `int` we use 32 bits (or 4 bytes) of memory.  
For a `int64`, we use 64 bits (or 8 bytes) of memory.  
Remember, a bit is the term for individual digits of a binary number.  

`1010` is four bits.  
`0001` is four bits.
`01` is two bits.  

The amount of bits is important because it dictates how many values you can represent.  
And the amount of bits is the same as the amount of "columns" the value has.  

What if we have 8 bits: `11110000`

Then we have 8 columns of numbers:

    128    64    32    16    8    4    2    1

We can represent up to 255 different values with 8 bits (or a byte).  




